<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/paperwork-review.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/paperwork-review-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/paperwork-review.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li aria-current="page">Claim glossary</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Reference</p><h1>Insurance Claim Appraisal Glossary</h1><p class="lede">41 terms you’ll meet in policies, estimates and appraisal awards, defined in plain English. Type in the search box to filter.</p></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><div class="gl-search"><label for="gloss-filter">Search the glossary</label><input id="gloss-filter" type="search" placeholder="Try “depreciation” or “umpire”" autocomplete="off"><p class="hint" data-gl-count>41 terms</p></div><div class="az"><a href="#g-A">A</a><a href="#g-B">B</a><a href="#g-C">C</a><a href="#g-D">D</a><a href="#g-E">E</a><a href="#g-F">F</a><a href="#g-H">H</a><a href="#g-I">I</a><a href="#g-L">L</a><a href="#g-M">M</a><a href="#g-O">O</a><a href="#g-P">P</a><a href="#g-R">R</a><a href="#g-S">S</a><a href="#g-U">U</a><a href="#g-X">X</a></div><dl class="glossary"><div class="gl-item" id="g-A"><dt>Actual cash value (ACV)</dt><dd>Replacement cost minus depreciation. Many policies pay ACV first, then pay the depreciation once repairs are made.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Additional living expense (ALE)</dt><dd>Coverage for the extra cost of living elsewhere while a home is being repaired after a covered loss. Often called Coverage D or loss of use.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Amount of loss</dt><dd>The dollar value of covered damage. This is what an appraisal panel decides.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Appraisal award</dt><dd>The written decision of an appraisal panel setting the amount of loss, signed by at least two of the three panel members.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Appraisal clause</dt><dd>The policy condition that lets either party demand appraisal when they disagree on the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Atmospheric river</dt><dd>A long, narrow band of moisture that brings heavy, sustained rain. Western Washington sees several each winter.</dd></div><div class="gl-item" id="g-B"><dt>Bomb cyclone</dt><dd>A storm whose pressure drops very quickly, producing strong winds. The November 2024 storm that hit Puget Sound was one.</dd></div><div class="gl-item" id="g-C"><dt>Code upgrade</dt><dd>Work needed to bring repairs up to current building codes. Usually paid under ordinance or law coverage.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Collapse</dt><dd>A sudden falling down of a building or part of it. Policies define it narrowly and cover it only in certain situations.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Contents</dt><dd>Personal belongings, covered under Coverage C in most homeowner policies.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Coverage A</dt><dd>Dwelling coverage for the house itself and attached structures.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Coverage B</dt><dd>Other structures coverage for detached garages, sheds, fences and similar.</dd></div><div class="gl-item" id="g-D"><dt>Debris removal</dt><dd>Coverage for removing damaged property and debris after a covered loss, often with a separate limit.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Declarations page</dt><dd>The summary page of your policy showing coverages, limits, deductibles and the policy period.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Depreciation</dt><dd>The reduction in value because of age, wear or obsolescence. Subtracted from replacement cost to get ACV.</dd></div><div class="gl-item" id="g-E"><dt>Earthquake deductible</dt><dd>A separate deductible for earthquake coverage, usually a percentage of the dwelling limit.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Extended replacement cost</dt><dd>An endorsement that pays above the dwelling limit, such as 25% or 50% more, if rebuilding costs exceed it.</dd></div><div class="gl-item" id="g-F"><dt>Flood exclusion</dt><dd>The standard policy exclusion for damage from surface water, rising rivers and similar. Flood needs separate coverage.</dd></div><div class="gl-item" id="g-H"><dt>HO-3</dt><dd>The most common homeowner policy form, covering the dwelling on an open-perils basis and contents on a named-perils basis.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>HO-6</dt><dd>A policy for condominium unit owners covering unit improvements, contents and liability.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Holdback</dt><dd>Recoverable depreciation the insurer withholds until repairs are completed.</dd></div><div class="gl-item" id="g-I"><dt>Ice dam</dt><dd>A ridge of ice at the roof edge that traps melting snow, which can back up under shingles and leak inside.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>IFCA</dt><dd>Washington’s Insurance Fair Conduct Act, which provides legal remedies for unreasonable denial of coverage or benefits.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Independent appraiser</dt><dd>An appraiser with no financial interest in the outcome or the repair contract, as most appraisal clauses require.</dd></div><div class="gl-item" id="g-L"><dt>Line item</dt><dd>A single entry in an estimate, such as removing and replacing a square of shingles.</dd></div><div class="gl-item" id="g-M"><dt>Master policy</dt><dd>The insurance policy a condominium or homeowners association buys to cover common areas and building elements.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Matching</dt><dd>Whether replacement materials must match undamaged ones so the repair looks uniform. A common source of dispute.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Mitigation</dt><dd>Steps taken to prevent further damage after a loss, such as tarping or water extraction. Policies require it.</dd></div><div class="gl-item" id="g-O"><dt>OIC</dt><dd>The Washington Office of the Insurance Commissioner, the state regulator of insurance companies and agents.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Ordinance or law coverage</dt><dd>Coverage for extra costs caused by building codes or laws when repairing or rebuilding.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Overhead and profit (O&amp;P)</dt><dd>A general contractor’s charge for coordinating multiple trades, often 10% overhead plus 10% profit.</dd></div><div class="gl-item" id="g-P"><dt>Proof of loss</dt><dd>A sworn statement of the amount claimed, which the insurer may request within a set time.</dd></div><div class="gl-item" id="g-R"><dt>Recoverable depreciation</dt><dd>Depreciation you can collect after completing repairs under a replacement cost policy.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Replacement cost value (RCV)</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace damaged property with materials of like kind and quality, without deducting depreciation.</dd></div><div class="gl-item" id="g-S"><dt>Scope of loss</dt><dd>The list of everything that is damaged and how it must be repaired. The foundation of any estimate.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Steep charge</dt><dd>Extra labor cost for working on roofs with a steep pitch, often 7/12 and above.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Suit limitation</dt><dd>The policy clause setting how long you have to file a lawsuit. Washington law sets a one-year minimum.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Supplement</dt><dd>A request to the insurer to add items or money to an estimate, supported by documentation.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Surplus lines</dt><dd>Insurance from carriers not licensed in the state but allowed to write coverage that the standard market won’t.</dd></div><div class="gl-item" id="g-U"><dt>Umpire</dt><dd>The neutral third member of an appraisal panel who decides items the two appraisers can’t agree on.</dd></div><div class="gl-item" id="g-X"><dt>Xactimate</dt><dd>Estimating software used by most insurers and contractors, with regional price lists.</dd></div></dl></article><aside class="side"><div class="side-in"><div class="side-box call-box"><h2 class="box-t">Talk it through, free</h2><p>Tell us what happened and what the insurer offered. We’ll tell you honestly whether appraisal fits.</p><a class="call-num" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-y full" href="/talk-to-an-appraiser/">Send claim details</a></div><div class="side-box"><h2 class="box-t">Resources</h2><ul class="side-links"><li><a href="/how-insurance-appraisal-works/">How appraisal works</a></li><li><a href="/claim-appraisal-faq/">Appraisal FAQ</a></li><li><a href="/claim-appraisal-glossary/" aria-current="page">Claim glossary</a></li><li><a href="/claim-resources/">Claim resources</a></li></ul></div></div></aside></div>
